<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Province of Hanover</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> created after Prussia annexed the Kingdom of Hanover in 1866, with its capital at Hanover. It covered most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and in 1922 absorbed the former Waldeck district of Pyrmont. It was dissolved after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when the British occupation authorities created the State of Hanover in 1946, merged into Lower Saxony later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/province-of-hanover.jpg" alt="Province of Hanover" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Hannover.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> tried to stay neutral, as did some other members of the German Confederation. After Hanover voted on 14 June 1866 in favour of mobilising confederation troops against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, Prussia took this as just cause for war. The kingdom was soon dissolved and annexed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> then used the private wealth of the dethroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a> to finance his continuing efforts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a>. The territory became the Province of Hanover, first of the Kingdom of Prussia and later of the Free State of Prussia.</p><p>In August 1946 the British military administration recreated the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> on the basis of the former kingdom. Three months later it was merged with Oldenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Schaumburg-Lippe into the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, with the city of Hanover as capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The province was divided into six regions, first called Landdrosteien (high bailiwicks): <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. On 1 April 1885 they were reorganised as Regierungsbezirke, the standard Prussian governorates, and subdivided into urban and rural counties, the old Amt structure being disbanded.</p><p>The boundaries shifted at times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a>, an urban county from 1919, was ceded to Oldenburg in 1937. In the same year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harburg-wilhelmsburg">Harburg-Wilhelmsburg</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuxhaven">Cuxhaven</a>, previously part of Hamburg, joined the province. In 1941 Goslar passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Holzminden came from it.</p><p>The central Prussian government appointed the heads of the province, called Oberpräsident. The first was Otto Graf zu Stolberg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> from 1867 to 1873, and Rudolf von Bennigsen served from 1888 to 1897. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-noske">Gustav Noske</a> of the SPD held the post from 1920 to 1933, followed by two members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">NSDAP</a>, Viktor Lutze and Hartmann Lauterbacher. Hinrich Wilhelm Kopf of the SPD held it in 1946. The elected provincial parliament, the Provinziallandtag, chose the executive, the Landesdirektor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Province of Hanover” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined Prussia's <b>Province of Hanover</b> in 1922 and is now Bad Pyrmont in Lower Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-pyrmont">County of Pyrmont</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1921 the inhabitants of the detached district of Pyrmont voted to join Prussia, and Pyrmont passed to the <b>Province of Hanover</b> in 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-waldeck">Free State of Waldeck</a></li></ul></section>
